6	 
7	 A browser multiplayer pirate treasure race. The game is specified in [`docs/spec.md`](docs/spec.md) (the text of `docs/spec.docx`); [`docs/architecture.md`](docs/architecture.md) maps this repository onto it.
8	 
9	-Browsers create or join a private match by a six-character code and sail placeholder ships around the slice sea: a compact map with four small islands with docks, reefs and shoal walls that funnel every route between islands through one central crossing, and eight outer-sea spawn slots. Up (or W) sails ahead, down (S) brakes and then reverses, left and right (A, D) turn, and E docks a ship beside a dock, at any speed. The authoritative server decides every position and sends each browser only the ships within its sight radius; the minimap's fog clears where the player has sailed.
10	+A match can be played and won end to end (§2, §5). Browsers create or join a private match by a six-character code and gather in its lobby, which lists the players; the host starts once two or more are in (late arrivals are refused). A 20-second countdown follows while the arena loads, then player control starts with 45 seconds of departure protection and the 35:00 timer counting down. The first player to hold every rubbing type is told the exact route to the final island (a compass, an arrow and a mark on the minimap), and everyone learns its sector in a banner; minute 20 and minute 28 bring more reveals. Stop inside the treasure circle holding every type and hold E for ten seconds to win: moving, letting go, losing a type, disconnecting or being grappled resets the claim, and any rival in range can grapple you. The match ends with the winner, or with no winner at the deadline, and every browser shows the results: each player's rubbings, thefts, losses, duels, gold, caches and islands.
11	+
12	+Ships sail placeholder hulls around the slice sea: a compact map with four small islands with docks, reefs and shoal walls that funnel every route between islands through one central crossing, and eight outer-sea spawn slots. Up (or W) sails ahead, down (S) brakes and then reverses, left and right (A, D) turn, and E docks a ship beside a dock, at any speed. The authoritative server decides every position and sends each browser only the ships within its sight radius; the minimap's fog clears where the player has sailed.
13	 
14	 Fights happen at sea (§6): J grapples the nearest ship ahead within six tiles. The attacker sees its aim, the target a warning with the side it comes from, and it lands after a one-second windup unless the target sails out of range or out of the aim (a miss costs six seconds). A grapple that lands stops both ships; after a three-second countdown the two fight the online duel inside the match, in the same page's arena, while everyone else in sight sees crossed swords and the elapsed time where they fight. The winner sails on with eight seconds of protection; the loser respawns at a dock 15 seconds after the fight, with 20 seconds of protection; a draw returns both to separate nearby points.
