2884	 - **Map format and pipeline.** The map is `assets/source/maps/slice-sea.tmj`, in a documented subset of Tiled's JSON format (`packages/content/src/tiledMap.ts`): orthogonal, finite, 24 px tiles (`TILE_PIXELS`, as drawn); one imageless tileset whose tiles carry a `type` of `sea`, `land`, `shoal` or `dock`; a `terrain` layer as a plain gid array; an `objects` layer with a named `island` rectangle per island (property `theme`), a `spawn` point per slot (properties `slot` and `facing`), one `hub` point and a `final-socket` rectangle per hidden final-island socket (properties `claimX`, `claimY`). Nothing else is accepted. `npm run map:build -- --from-sketch` writes it from one mirrored ASCII quarter (`sliceSeaSketch.ts`; a unit test fails when they differ). `npm run map:build` (`scripts/build-map.ts`) converts it into `packages/content/src/maps/sliceSea.ts` (terrain rows and objects) if it passes the validators; a unit test fails when that module is stale (it reads the `.tmj` as a Vite `?raw` import).
2885	 - **Validators.** `validateSeaMap` checks §2 and §3: four small islands, each land tile inside one island rectangle, each island with a dock beside its land and open sea; shoals; eight spawn slots on open sea outside the islands' bounding box and pairwise beyond `ENGAGEMENT_RANGE_TILES` (6); every dock and slot reachable by sea; and, by eight-way shortest paths (no cut corners) at full speed: each island's nearest neighbour 20–40 s away, no dock-or-slot crossing over 90 s, a dock within 40 s of every slot. No empty stretches: every navigable tile is within the sight radius of land, a shoal or a dock (not the edge). Routes meet: closing the disc around the hub (open sea near the centre; radius half the sight radius, so ships in it see each other) lengthens or cuts every shortest path between docks of different islands. At most 112 tiles a side. The slice sea (111 × 111 tiles, midline shoal walls open only in the hub's disc): neighbours 26 s apart, longest crossing 39 s, water within 9.9 tiles of an obstacle, 90 % open sea.
2886	-- **Final-island sockets (§3, §5).** `map.finalSockets` holds four identical dockless islets (one sketched islet, mirrored), each a rectangle of at most 6 × 6 and a claim tile: open sea beside it whose centre is the claim point, the treasure circle's centre (`TREASURE_CIRCLE_RADIUS_TILES`). Their land belongs to no island, so docks, sailing, respawn and visibility treat them as plain land. `validateFinalSockets` (run by `validateSeaMap`) requires every claim tile within 90 s of every spawn slot, at least two sockets in any sector holding one (`sectorOf`: column ⌊3x / width⌋, row ⌊3y / height⌋, the grid a sector reveal must use), at least 12 navigable tiles in each circle, and nothing within 8 tiles of what the running tests sail on (`keepClearZones`: the hub, spawn slots, docks and 14 tiles out from each, the out-of-sight row). The slice's sockets sit two in the north-centre sector and two in the south-centre one. `chooseFinalSocket(map, secret)` picks a match's socket from a 32-bit secret the server draws from a cryptographic source, mixed by the murmur3 finaliser before the modulo; never pass the public match seed (§3). Nothing uses the choice yet.
2887	+- **Final-island sockets (§3, §5).** `map.finalSockets` holds four identical dockless islets (one sketched islet, mirrored), each a rectangle of at most 6 × 6 and a claim tile: open sea beside it whose centre is the claim point, the treasure circle's centre (`TREASURE_CIRCLE_RADIUS_TILES`). Their land belongs to no island, so docks, sailing, respawn and visibility treat them as plain land. `validateFinalSockets` (run by `validateSeaMap`) requires every claim tile within 90 s of every spawn slot, at least two sockets in any sector holding one (`sectorOf`: column ⌊3x / width⌋, row ⌊3y / height⌋, the grid a sector reveal must use), at least 12 navigable tiles in each circle, and nothing within 8 tiles of what the running tests sail on (`keepClearZones`: the hub, spawn slots, docks and 14 tiles out from each, the out-of-sight row). The slice's sockets sit two in the north-centre sector and two in the south-centre one. `chooseFinalSocket(map, secret)` picks a match's socket from a 32-bit secret, mixed by the murmur3 finaliser before the modulo; the room passes the final-socket seed derived from its master secret (below), never the public match seed (§3).
2888	 - **Sailing.** Positions are integer sub-units (256 a tile); headings are 64 directions, 0 east, clockwise (y grows down), unit vectors from a literal cosine table. Each world step the throttle (`-moveY`) accelerates towards 39 sub-units a step (3.05 tiles a second), brakes, or reverses slowly from a stop; `moveX` turns a heading a step (3.2 s a circle); none coasts to a stop. On 24 px tiles that handles like the old five tiles a second on 16 px (unit tests hold it within 25 %). The hull is a square of half-size 80. A move sweeps each axis in turn through the tile columns or rows its leading edge enters and stops flush at the first land, shoal or map edge: a ship slides along an obstacle met at an angle, runs aground head-on, and never tunnels.
2889	 - **Docking.** Hold E near a dock to moor, at any speed and throttle: with the hull on or beside the berth tile (unless pinched), or within reach with a clear run, it moves to the dock's centre, stops and records the dock, so no arrival slides past or grounds beside it (pinched: a diagonal tile whose two shared tiles are blocked). It stays moored while E or the throttle it moored with (`mooredThrottle`) is held; pressing a throttle after releasing that one, or the other direction, casts off. An input stall does not: the server gives a lapsed client no input, keeping the latch. The keyboard keeps a tap of E until its next read (not past a blur), the server until its next step; then the HUD says "Press E to dock".
2890	 - **Visibility boundary (§16).** The room's `ships` map is tagged `.view()`. Each client gets a `StateView`; after every mirror the room puts into it the client's own ship and each ship within `SIGHT_RADIUS_TILES` (10, `visibleShipIds`) and takes out the rest, so Colyseus encodes no other ship into that client's patches (an integration test checks the decoded state and the raw frames). `players` in the state is the public player count. Terrain is static content every client bundles, never synced; the minimap fog is client-side presentation, not a security boundary.
2891	 - **Test placement.** Tests place ships through `POST /__test/place` (code, session id, tile, heading); its tile bounds come from the map. Only the dev and test entry (`apps/server/src/dev.ts`) offers it, when `PIRATE_SEA_RACE_TEST_PLACEMENT=1`; the production entry (`main.ts`) never reads that, which the build smoke test checks. It only moves a ship: admission and views are unchanged.
2892	-- **Test-only timings and source layout.** `startGameServer({ matchTuning })` gives every match of that server other rules (a short seat reservation, respawn wait or duel absence) or a pinned source layout; integration tests use it and assert in world ticks. Colyseus has one matchmaker per process, so each integration test file has one set of timings. The dev and test entry also reads `PIRATE_SEA_RACE_TEST_SOURCES` (for example `sun,sun|moon,moon|sun,sun|moon,moon`: the types of each island's sources), which the browser tests set so a scenario picks an island by type. `main.ts` reads neither (the build smoke test starts it with a malformed layout set) and turns the per-tick invariant checks off.
2893	+- **Test-only timings and source layout.** `startGameServer({ matchTuning })` gives every match of that server other rules (a short seat reservation, respawn wait, duel absence, ready countdown, protection, match, channel or reveals), a pinned source layout, a pinned final-socket secret, an injected master secret, or the room's test hooks; integration tests use it and assert in world ticks. Colyseus has one matchmaker per process, so each integration test file has one set of timings. The dev and test entry also reads `PIRATE_SEA_RACE_TEST_SOURCES` (for example `sun,sun|moon,moon|sun,sun|moon,moon`: the types of each island's sources), `PIRATE_SEA_RACE_TEST_TIMINGS` (for example `ready=60,protection=20,match=12000`, in world ticks; also `sectors` and `carriers`) and `PIRATE_SEA_RACE_TEST_FINAL_SECRET` (a 32-bit secret), which the browser tests set so a scenario picks an island by type, plays a whole match in a minute and knows where the treasure is. All are off by default. `main.ts` reads none (the build smoke test starts it with all of them set, malformed, and checks the 20 s countdown and 35:00 timer) and turns the per-tick invariant checks off.
2894	 
2895	 ## Duels at sea (§6, §14)
2896	 
2897	-- **Encounters in the room.** Each world step (20 Hz), after sailing, `MatchRoom` builds one tick's snapshot for `stepEncounters` (`docs/encounters.md`): every ship's position and heading, whether its player is connected, pressed J since the last world step (the input gate latches it like E), has a loaded arena, or started a collection or cache claim last tick (which ends protection). It passes back the reports of duels that ended since the last tick, and a settlement only once the ledger has settled the duel (below). It applies the events: `duel-reserved` stops both ships and opens a duel; `duel-started` starts its fight; a ready-check `duel-forfeited` or `duel-cancelled` ends it; `player-returned` and `player-respawned` move a ship (at rest, or moored at the dock). Everything else (attempts, protection, cooldowns, the loser's wait) is mirrored from the rules' state. The rules alone choose targets and decide hits, returns and docks; the room never re-implements them. Reserved and respawning ships get neutral input, so they stay put. Departure protection needs a match start, which private matches do not have yet, so no one starts protected.
2898	+- **Encounters in the room.** Each world step (20 Hz), after sailing, `MatchRoom` builds one tick's snapshot for `stepEncounters` (`docs/encounters.md`): every ship's position and heading, whether its player is connected, pressed J since the last world step (the input gate latches it like E), has a loaded arena, or started a collection or cache claim last tick (which ends protection). It passes back the reports of duels that ended since the last tick, and a settlement only once the ledger has settled the duel (below). It applies the events: `duel-reserved` stops both ships and opens a duel; `duel-started` starts its fight; a ready-check `duel-forfeited` or `duel-cancelled` ends it; `player-returned` and `player-respawned` move a ship (at rest, or moored at the dock). Everything else (attempts, protection, cooldowns, the loser's wait) is mirrored from the rules' state. The rules alone choose targets and decide hits, returns and docks; the room never re-implements them. Reserved and respawning ships get neutral input, so they stay put. The rules run from control start, when every ship gets the departure's protection, to the match's end.
2899	 - **One duel machine.** `DuelMachine` (`apps/server/src/duelMachine.ts`) is the duel both rooms run: seats and their `DuelInputGate`s, the 180-frame countdown with round-trip probes and the input delay chosen 30 frames before its end, `DuelAuthority` stepped at 60 Hz with a bundle every third frame, the reconnection bookkeeping (a dropped seat is marked away, a returning one gets a snapshot bundle) and the forfeits decided together at the next frame boundary. It writes a lobby schema and sends through a callback, so the host room owns clients and timers. `DuelRoom` starts the fight when the countdown ends (`start: "countdown"`); the match room starts it when the rules say so (`start: "called"`: the countdown is over and both fighters sent `duel-ready`). A countdown that ends with a fighter still loading waits in `waiting`, without probes; clients drop their session then, so the fight's clock starts from the fight's own frames. The match room keeps each duel's lobby for 2 s after settlement (`DUEL_RESULT_TICKS`), so both read the result.

